Missing: A rename does not edit a function name that a macro stringizes, e.g. Cgreen's expect()

Cgreen's expect(f, ...) passes f to STRINGIFY_TOKEN(token), which is #token. In the
source, f is an identifier with a position. The # turns it into a string during the
expansion, in the stringize branch of the macro body expansion in src/yylex.c
(macroArgumentsToString()). Only the STRING_LITERAL reaches the parser, so no reference
is recorded. A rename of the function leaves expect(helper) as it was, and the test
that used it then fails at run time.

The likely fix: when # stringizes an argument that is one identifier, and a symbol of
that name is visible, record a reference to that symbol at the position of the
identifier in the source. The rename then edits the identifier, and the string follows
on the next expansion. The rule is not specific to Cgreen.

To decide or check first:
- The stringize branch uses the position of the parameter in the macro definition. The
  reference needs the position of the actual argument, through one extra macro level
  (expect -> STRINGIFY_TOKEN).
- The lookup happens in the lexer. Check that a symbol in the current scope can be
  looked up there.
- In assert_that(x, ...), x is also used unstringized, so the two references at the
  same position must become one.

doc/docs/11-planned-features.adoc, "Names in stringized macro arguments", has the
background (Mac, session f5b5c93a, 2026-11-24).
